    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Electron Affinity",
    "reading_order" : 68,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000069",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The **electron affinity** (Δ<sub>*ea*</sub>*H*) of an element is the energy change when **one mole **of electrons is gained by **one mole** of gaseous atoms of an element to form **one mole **of gaseous ions under standard conditions",
    "reading_order" : 69,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000070",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "For example, the first electron affinity of chlorine is:",
    "reading_order" : 70,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000071",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Cl (g)+ e**<sup>**–**</sup>** → Cl**<sup>**-**</sup>** (g)          Δ**<sub>***ea***</sub>***H***<sup>**ꝋ **</sup>**= -364 kJ mol**<sup>**-1**</sup>",
    "reading_order" : 71,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000072",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The **first electron affinity** is always **exothermic** as energy is released when electrons are attracted to the atoms",
    "reading_order" : 72,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000073",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "However, the **second electron affinity** of an element can be **endothermic **as illustrated by oxygen:",
    "reading_order" : 73,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000074",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**O**<sup>**– **</sup>**(g) + e**<sup>**–**</sup>** → O**<sup>**2-**</sup>** (g)          Δ**<sub>***ea***</sub>***H***<sup>**ꝋ **</sup>**= +844 kJ mol**<sup>**-1**</sup>",
    "reading_order" : 74,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000075",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "This is because a large force of **repulsion** must be overcome between the negatively charged ion and the second electron requiring a large input of energy",
    "reading_order" : 75,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  } ],
